Important finding: yoyo doesn't currently use these queues at all, and they're **user-injected pending messages**, not a "model has more to do" signal. The queues represent messages a user queued while the agent was working (steering/follow-up injection). So `follow_up_queue_snapshot()` being non-empty means the user queued more input — that IS a signal there's more to do, but it's a different mechanism than "the model was cut off mid-work."

This is a subtle but important scoping insight. The issue's premise (queues give "a real, authoritative view of pending/queued work") is partially right: a non-empty follow-up queue does mean more work is pending, and if the queue is non-empty we should continue regardless of the heuristic. But an empty queue does NOT mean the model finished — so we still need looks_incomplete as the fallback. This matches the issue's own design (queue path, fall back to heuristic).

